WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:06.050
Spark gives you a hundred executors. Then one group by, and the whole job slows to a crawl.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.780
Picture a billion orders, spread across those executors.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.680
You run: df.groupBy("country").count()

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:25.310
To count by country, every row with the same key has to land in the same partition.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.400
So data moves between executors. That movement is called the shuffle.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:33.250
Follow one record.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:34.300
Serialize it.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:35.660
Write it to disk.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.360
Send it over the network to another executor.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.060
The next stage fetches it.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:44.380
Deserializes it.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:46.040
And finally aggregates.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:53.770
Every one of those steps costs time. The next stage can't start until all the data arrives, so its tasks just sit and wait.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:00.440
The fix? Move less data. Or spread the work across more executors.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.990
Filter rows and columns before the shuffle.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.160
And avoid shuffles you don't need.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:16.570
One powerful trick: broadcast the small table to every executor, and skip the shuffle entirely.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.620
Spark performance. Episode three.

